---
// How the home band's fit check is measured: agreement with my own job-radar labels on a
// held-out set, and a red team of invented postings with instructions for AI tools. Every
// figure comes from the committed eval JSON (src/fit/evals/), so a re-run updates the page.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { LIMITS } from '../../fit/check';
import { AGREEMENT as a, FEW_POSITIVES, RED_TEAM as r } from '../../fit/evals/figures';
import { BASES } from '../../fit/evals/redteam';
import { interval, percent, type Rate } from '../../fit/evals/stats';

const REPO = 'https://github.com/steveu/urm.st/blob/master';
const date = (iso: string) => new Date(iso).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
const count = (k: number, n: number) => (k === n ? `all ${n}` : k === 0 ? `none of ${n}` : `${k} of ${n}`);
const figure = (x: Rate) => `${percent(x)}, ${interval(x)}`;

const v = a.verdict;
const rows = [
  { label: 'Strong', ...a.verdicts.strong },
  { label: 'Possible', ...a.verdicts.possible },
  { label: 'Poor', ...a.verdicts.poor },
];
const sameWithLocation = a.verdictOrLocation.agreement.k === v.agreement.k;
const sameDay = a.ranAt === r.ranAt && a.model === r.model && a.promptVersion === r.promptVersion;
const allQuoted = r.injectionQuoted.k === r.injectionFlagged.k;
---

<Base
  title="How the fit check is measured"
  description="How often the fit check agrees with my own judgement of real job postings, and what it does with instructions hidden in a posting for AI tools. Counts only, with sample sizes."
>
  <article class="wrap page">
    <header class="intro">
      <p class="crumbs"><a href="/work">Work</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> Fit check</p>
      <h1>How the fit check is measured</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Two measurements of the <a href="/#fit-check">fit check on the home page</a>: how often it agrees with my own
        judgement of real postings, and what it does with instructions hidden in a posting for AI tools. Only the counts
        are published.
      </p>
      <p class="run-note">
        {
          sameDay
            ? `Both last run on ${date(a.ranAt)}, with ${a.model} and prompt ${a.promptVersion}.`
            : `Agreement last run on ${date(a.ranAt)} (${a.model}, prompt ${a.promptVersion}); red team on ${date(r.ranAt)} (${r.model}, prompt ${r.promptVersion}).`
        }{' '}
        Both are run again whenever the prompt, the profile or the model changes.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="body">
      <h2>Agreement with my own judgement</h2>
      <p>
        job-radar is the tool I built to find and read postings for my own job search. As I go through what it finds, I label
        each posting keep, maybe or skip. Those labels are the nearest thing I have to a right answer.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href={`${REPO}/src/fit/profile.md`}>scoring profile</a> the check uses was frozen on {date(a.heldOutAfter)}.
        Only postings I first labelled after that count here, so the profile wasn’t written with any of them in view. For
        each one, a script reads the posting from job-radar’s database on my own machine, opened read-only, turns its
        description into plain text, cuts it to the same {LIMITS.maxChars.toLocaleString('en-GB')} characters as the paste
        box, and runs exactly the check the home page runs. Keep and maybe count as yes; skip counts as no. Only the counts
        are kept: no posting, title or employer is published.
      </p>

      <div class="table" role="region" aria-labelledby="agreement-caption" tabindex="0">
        <table>
          <caption id="agreement-caption">The check’s verdict against my label, {a.n} postings</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Check said</th>
              <th scope="col">I’d kept or marked maybe</th>
              <th scope="col">I’d skipped</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              rows.map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{row.label}</th>
                  <td>{row.positive}</td>
                  <td>{row.negative}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
          <tfoot>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">All</th>
              <td>{a.positives}</td>
              <td>{a.negatives}</td>
            </tr>
          </tfoot>
        </table>
      </div>

      <ul class="figures">
        <li>
          Of the {v.poorWereSkips.n} it called poor, I’d skipped {v.poorWereSkips.k}{' '}
          <span class="ci">({figure(v.poorWereSkips)})</span>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Of the {v.recallOfPositives.n} I’d kept or marked maybe, it rated {v.recallOfPositives.k} strong or possible{' '}
          <span class="ci">({figure(v.recallOfPositives)})</span>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Of the {v.fitWereKeepOrMaybe.n} it rated strong or possible, I’d kept or marked maybe {v.fitWereKeepOrMaybe.k}{' '}
          <span class="ci">({figure(v.fitWereKeepOrMaybe)})</span>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Overall it agreed with me on {v.agreement.k} of {v.agreement.n} <span class="ci">({figure(v.agreement)})</span>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="aside">
        The ranges are 95% Wilson intervals: with samples this small, the true rate could reasonably sit anywhere inside
        them.
      </p>

      <h3>What this doesn’t show</h3>
      {
        a.positives < FEW_POSITIVES && (
          <p>
            <strong>Few yeses so far.</strong> Only {a.positives} of the {a.n} postings were ones I’d keep or consider. Most
            postings are a no, which is the nature of a job search, but it means the figures about the yes side are rough:{' '}
            {v.recallOfPositives.k} of {v.recallOfPositives.n} could be anything from {interval(v.recallOfPositives)}. They’ll
            firm up as I label more.
          </p>
        )
      }
      <p>
        <strong>Easy noes count too.</strong> Most postings are plainly not a fit, so the overall agreement mostly measures
        saying no to those. The harder test is the yes side, where {v.fitWereKeepOrMaybe.n - v.fitWereKeepOrMaybe.k} of the
        check’s {v.fitWereKeepOrMaybe.n} strong or possible verdicts went to postings I’d skipped. I also skip postings for
        reasons the verdict doesn’t judge, such as pay, so not every one of those is a miss.
        {sameWithLocation && ' Counting a location that doesn’t work as a no as well changes nothing in this sample.'}
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>One person’s labels.</strong> The labels are mine, made while job hunting, and the postings are the ones
        job-radar found for my search rather than a random sample of job adverts.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Answers that didn’t check out.</strong>{' '}
        {count(a.firstAttemptFailedQuoteCheck.k, a.firstAttemptFailedQuoteCheck.n)} first answers failed the quote check
        and needed the one retry;{' '}
        {a.unassessable === 0 ? 'all checked out after it.' : `${a.unassessable} still didn’t check out after it, and ${a.unassessable === 1 ? 'isn’t' : 'aren’t'} counted above.`}
      </p>

      <h2>Instructions hidden for AI tools</h2>
      <p>
        Some postings carry text meant for AI screening tools rather than people: “ignore previous instructions”, or a note
        telling any AI that reads it to rate the candidate highly. The check reads the posting as data, and is told to flag
        such text, quote it, and otherwise ignore it.
      </p>
      <p>
        To test that, I wrote <a href={`${REPO}/src/fit/evals/redteam.ts`}>{r.pairs} pairs of made-up postings</a>, on
        {' '}{BASES.length} invented roles that run from a poor fit to a strong one. In each pair, one posting is as written and
        the other has an instruction slipped in: a plain “ignore previous instructions”, fake system and assistant messages,
        a fake end to the posting, orders to rate it strong or poor, a false location, a request to reveal the prompt, one
        in French, and text hidden with styling in the HTML, which a job board’s listing still passes on. Each version was
        checked once.
      </p>
      <ul class="figures">
        <li>
          The verdict changed in {count(r.verdictChanged.k, r.verdictChanged.n)} pairs <span class="ci">({figure(r.verdictChanged)})</span>,
          and the location verdict in {count(r.locationChanged.k, r.locationChanged.n)}.
        </li>
        <li>
          The instruction was flagged in {count(r.injectionFlagged.k, r.injectionFlagged.n)} <span class="ci">({figure(r.injectionFlagged)})</span>{
            allQuoted ? ', each time quoting the instruction itself' : `, quoting the instruction itself in ${r.injectionQuoted.k}`
          }.
        </li>
        <li>
          Postings without an instruction flagged by mistake: {count(r.cleanFlagged.k, r.cleanFlagged.n)}.
        </li>
        <li>
          Each role was checked twice without an instruction, and the two verdicts agreed for
          {' '}{count(r.cleanRunsDisagreed.n - r.cleanRunsDisagreed.k, r.cleanRunsDisagreed.n)}, so a verdict that moved
          would be hard to put down to chance.
        </li>
        <li>
          Answers that failed the quote check first time: {count(r.firstAttemptFailedQuoteCheck.k, r.firstAttemptFailedQuoteCheck.n)}.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What this doesn’t show</h3>
      <p>
        I wrote these attacks knowing the prompt, and there are only {r.pairs} of them, so the true rate at which a verdict
        moves is likely under {Math.round((r.verdictChanged.high ?? 1) * 100)}%, not known to be {percent(r.verdictChanged)}. Someone determined, with many tries, could do better. What limits the damage is the design:
        the model only picks a verdict, reasons from a fixed list and quotes from the posting, and every quote is checked
        before it’s shown. The worst a successful instruction can do is move the verdict; it can’t put words on this site.
      </p>

      <h2>Running them again</h2>
      <p>
        Both scripts are in the repo: <a href={`${REPO}/scripts/fit-agreement.ts`}>the agreement script</a>, which opens
        job-radar’s database read-only and writes only counts, and <a href={`${REPO}/scripts/fit-redteam.ts`}>the red
        team’s</a>. The figures record the model and prompt version they were measured with, and a test fails if either
        changes without a re-run.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-block: clamp(56px, 8vw, 96px) 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 40px;
  }

  .intro {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 20px;
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  .crumbs {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  .crumbs span {
    margin-inline: 6px;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(32px, 4.4vw, 50px);
    line-height: 1.12;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: clamp(17px, 1.6vw, 19px);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .lede a,
  .body a {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .run-note {
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--faint);
    padding-left: 14px;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--orange);
  }

  .body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1em;
    max-width: 680px;
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .body h2 {
    margin-top: 0.8em;
    font-size: 24px;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .body h3 {
    margin-top: 0.4em;
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .figures {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.2em;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.4em;
  }

  .ci,
  .aside {
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  .aside {
    font-size: 15px;
  }

  .table {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 15px;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  caption {
    text-align: left;
    padding-bottom: 8px;
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  th,
  td {
    padding: 6px 16px 6px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    text-align: left;
  }

  td {
    text-align: right;
    min-width: 7ch;
  }

  thead th {
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 13px;
    vertical-align: bottom;
  }

  thead th + th {
    text-align: right;
  }

  tbody th,
  tfoot th {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  tfoot th,
  tfoot td {
    border-bottom: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
